<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hooghly River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hooghly is a distributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, flowing about 260 kilometres south past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. In the 17th and 18th centuries its banks were lined with European trading settlements, among them Portuguese Bandel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinsurah">Chinsurah</a>, French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandernagore</a>, Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> and British Calcutta. It remains an important waterway for the port of Kolkata.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hooghly-river.jpg" alt="Hooghly River" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greg O'Beirne · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HooghlyRiverOverBally_gobeirne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
